hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the key  concerns of Rene Descartes meditations  is being able to distinguish the true  from the false and these are terms that  we use quite easily without sometimes  understanding precisely what we mean  when we say that something is false or  that something is true and in meditation  3 this is going to play an important  role and get some preliminary  clarification on the part of Descartes  he's going to make some important  distinctions about the ways in which  something can be said to be false now  this is incredibly important because the  entire Cartesian method of hyperbolic or  methodological or metaphysical doubt  however you want to describe it depended  on treating things that are doubtful as  if they are indeed false so in what  sense are those things false about  midway through the third meditation  Descartes talks about a distinction  between being false in terms of true and  formal falsity and material falsity now  he's using  rather scholastic terminology there but  I think you can see what he means  quite quite easily when we look at what  he says so he says as I remarked earlier  it's only in judgments that one can find  true and formal falsity and now that's  kind of an interesting way to talk about  it true falsity falsity in the truest  sense falsity you could say in its  essential pattern or or nature and so  he's saying this relies on us making  judgments and what is a judgment a  judgment is an affirmation or denial of  something being the case it has you  could say a propositional content it  affirms or denies something of something  and it's an act that we engage in with  their mind whether we do so merely in  thought or do so linguistically it is a  complex thing so it is connecting up  several different ideas and making some  sort of affirmation or denial that is  where genuine falsity or formal falsity  or true falsity resides for Descartes  now he talks then about a material  falsity as well and he says that we can  still nevertheless find in ideas a  certain material falsity that is when  they represent what is not or what is  nothing as if it was something and here  he's kind of speaking in shorthand he  talks about something that's nothing  Karianna but we could also think of that  this as talking about something that is  other that is different right so when  ideas represent things for example if I  have an idea  a unicorn and then I tack on as some  people have done and  I remember this is from the 70s and 80s  they would add some Pegasus wings to it  and somebody had come along and said  that's not a real unicorn  well a unicorn isn't real anyway there's  a material falsity to unicorn a horse  with a horn on it just as much as there  is if you add some wings to it right and  may as well you know color them  different colors and make them rainbow  we can imagine whatever we want when it  comes to unicorns right but the idea  doesn't represent the reality and in  that sense you can say that it has a  certain material falsity there's a  disconnect in what the idea is  representing and whether there's any  such thing like that in existence now a  little bit earlier you notice the cart  here referred back to a distinction that  he was making this is earlier in the  third meditation he says that if we  think about the thoughts that we have  right and where we can find truth and  falsity he says that among my thoughts  some are like images of things and those  are the ones that we should properly  call ideas because they represent things  like like a human being or a chimera or  the sky or an angel or even God and then  he says of the others beyond this they  have different forms for example when I  will or wish when I fear when I affirm  and when I deny I conceive he says  something like the subject of the action  of my mind but I add also something by  that action to the idea that I have of  that thing and of this genre of thoughts  he says some of these are called  Volition's willings votantes right or  affections meaning emotions desires all  the  sorts of effective states and others are  called judgments so we have now three  different types of thoughts we have  ideas properly speaking representing  things we have affections and Volition's  and then we have the judgments that we  just talked about so he tells us where  are we gonna find falsity in ideas now  we just talked about them not  representing things accurately as they  are and in that we can talk about a  certain material falsity considered  solely as ideas considered in themselves  as images that are presented to us there  is no falsity to them we are in fact  truly experiencing them whether it be  through what we take to be sense  perception or imagination or even if you  know we're doing something intellectual  with the understanding it that would be  the case as well so he says so for what  concerns ideas if we consider them in  solely in themselves and we don't  connect them to some other thing they  can't properly speaking be false  there is no falsity in them except in  this material sense of not corresponding  to something else consider just as the  ideas or images that we're being  presented with they're completely true  and it's also true to say I have these  ideas I am experiencing these ideas I am  imagining this what about our affections  and Volition's can we speak of them  being true or false Descartes says yes  we can and and there's a long tradition  going back quite a ways of saying maybe  when it comes to emotions and choices or  decisions we can find some criteria by  which to call them true or false in some  practical sense right Descartes says we  don't have to fear either that we would  encounter falsity in a fact  or Volition's why not why can't my  Volition's be false in the sense of  being wrong willing the wrong thing  right he says well because even though I  can desire bad things or even what will  never come to pass  nevertheless it is for that no less true  that I do in fact desire them so if  we're considering Volition's we're  considering desires we're considering  emotional reactions as the ideas that we  actually have within ourselves  there's nothing false about them it's  only insofar as we relate them somehow  to something else and this is why  judgment is so absolutely central when  it comes to falsity for Descartes what  goes on in a judgment as I remarked  earlier judgments are competent they  involve affirming and denying which  remember were among the modes of thought  that Descartes said when what am i a  thinking substance I am a being that  thinks I am a being that imagines I'm a  being that wills I'm also a being that  affirms and denies and affirming and  denying is engaging in judgment so I  says so all that remains are judgments  in which I must take guard cautiously  not to fool myself because the principle  error and the most common one that I'm  going to encounter consists in that I  judge that the ideas that I have in  myself are resembling or in conformity  you could say corresponding to the  things that are outside of myself that  is the common judgment that I'm making  all of the time and that is a judgment  that's not simply registering something  that is the mind saying I have this idea  this idea corresponds to something out  there  have an idea of a book it's this book  alright this object that I'm perceiving  you're perceiving now as well as an idea  so he says certainly if I consider just  the ideas like certain modes or fashions  of my thought without wanting or willing  choosing to connect them to something  else that's exterior then I have no  occasion to go astray  I'm not affirming that they actually do  correspond to things or denying that so  this is an important point and what's  going on metaphysically when there's  falsity in our our judgment in our grasp  on things or even in the material  falsity about the idea is not actually  connecting up with the things that that  are so you talks about ideas that  represent things that are not and here  we we might think about this in several  different ways the cart is running  through this rather summarily but we  should distinguish here several  different ways in which we might have  ideas of things that are are not right  one would be that we have an idea of  something that simply is not the case at  all doesn't exist period so again the  Unicorn  or the square circle you know  absurdities things along those lines  then we might also have ideas about  things that we represent to ourselves in  ways that don't actually depict the  thing that that's out there so for  example when we are thinking about human  beings who we encounter there is a  possibility that cart considers that  maybe they're not genuinely human beings  but machines that clothing is is placed  upon and they're there to trick us or  something like that in that case we  would have something we would have an  idea and the idea would be in some way  connected to a reality or an external  thing but the external thing and the  idea don't actually map on  to each other right so we don't have a  total lack of existence we have a  non-existing in this way and then he  uses the example of heat and coldness  and he says these are we take these for  granted but these are actually rather  unclear and obscure ideas and maybe they  don't reflect anything genuinely out  there I mean we we nowadays think that  it has to do with you know the vibration  of of atoms right and how fast they're  going and and we think about heat in  that way we think about radiant energy  but with the cart with what he's working  with he's saying listen the ideas that  we actually have of heat and cold you  know we we can have these ideas but do  they actually map onto anything perhaps  not because they're confused in  themselves so where do we get this  falsity or confusion from here's where  Descartes says something really quite  interesting metaphysically and it would  be very easy for a reader to pass over  this this is something that is central  to the Cartesian metaphysics he says  that they proceed or arise from  nothingness nay onth is the word that  he's using there and what does that  really mean he says that they are in me  this is the formulation because of a  lack a monk or an imperfection within me  so the nothingness is not somewhere out  there right the the nothingness is  actually within myself there's some some  sort of privation or something that's  that that ought to be there that isn't  there or just doesn't happen to be there  and that's why we get things wrong when  we get them wrong now that that  nothingness or or privation generally is  going to take the form of the judgments  that we're making and the last thing  I'll say about this is Descartes thinks  that we're making judgments much more  often than we actually realize that  we're doing and part of the Cartesian  meditations is to help us attend to  those judgments that we are in fact  making so we can determine where falsity  actually resides the answer to that is  in its proper sense in our judgments not  in the ideas themselves not in the  thoughts that we call affections or  Volition's but in that that species of  thought that we call judgment that  consists in affirming and denying  you
 

